an!\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My teammate (Previous Player) just played a ♠A to beat the Landlord\'s ♦K. This is a strong control play by my teammate. I hold a 2 and the Rocket (Double Joker), which gives me great endgame power, but my hand has many small singles (3, 5, 6, 7, 9, Q) and loose structure (breaking pairs for a straight leaves more singles). Beating my teammate\'s Ace with my 2 would be a mistake because: 1) It wastes a high control card on a teammate; 2) It puts me on lead with a weak hand structure (I would likely have to lead a small single like ♠3, which the Landlord can easily exploit); 3) It prevents the Landlord from being forced to use a 2 to beat the Ace. I should let the Ace stand to test the Landlord\'s strength or let my teammate continue if the Landlord passes.\n\nReason: Beating a teammate\'s high card (Ace) is generally bad strategy unless you can win immediately or set up a guaranteed win. I have a messy hand with many singles, so taking the lead is disadvantageous. I have the Rocket to defend later if the Landlord takes control with a 2.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
